---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

// A translation, not a paraphrase: every paragraph of the russian original is
// here in the same order. The original was written up by an editor from his
// telling, so the byline says so rather than claiming it as his writing.
const title = "my only english teacher was a homeless man on the town square";
const description =
  "how dmytro klochko got to upper intermediate in three months. skyeng magazine, translated.";
---

<Layout title={`${title} · dmytro klochko`} description={description} current="home">
  <section class="shell session piece" data-scene="fire" aria-labelledby="w1">
    <p class="piece-up">
      <a href="/writing"><span class="arw" aria-hidden="true">↖</span>writing</a>
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <h1 class="card-title" id="w1">{title}</h1>
    </div>

    <p class="lede">how i got to upper intermediate in three months.</p>

    <p class="piece-by">
      as told to anna dubinskaya, skyeng magazine.{" "}
      <a
        href="https://skyeng.ru/magazine/moj-edinstvennyj-uchitel-anglijskogo-bomzh-na-gorodskoj-ploshadi/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
      >the original, in russian<span class="arw">↗</span></a>
    </p>

    <div class="prose piece-body">
      <p>
        a small town, no experience, a salary of 15,000 roubles and not knowing
        even the word <i>door</i>: none of that stops a career. it turns out you
        can learn english in three months and get a job at a tech company without
        moving to moscow.
      </p>
      <p>
        i didn't start from zero, i started from below it. i finished school in
        donetsk and then the war started. my mother got ill, my father crawled to
        the plant under shelling, and then that work was gone too. i moved to kyiv
        and lived on aid packages there, bread with condensed milk, sometimes the
        only thing i ate all day. i got into the kyiv-mohyla academy, the best
        university in ukraine. it was funny: i came to class and announced
        proudly, "i've already had 72 hours of english in a year." turned out they
        did 144 a trimester, and in the first class the teachers said, "ohhh, well,
        we understand you now."
      </p>
      <p>
        then i was riding the metro and suddenly thought: how do you say{" "}
        <i>door</i> in english? i don't know. <i>floor</i>? don't know that either.
        and i started translating words right there on my phone. for some reason it
        made me wildly happy and curious. a couple of days later i started losing
        my mind and spending an unreasonable amount of time on english: downloading
        endless apps to burn through every free trial, watching series, skipping
        classes.
      </p>
      <p>
        every discovery in game of thrones delighted me. <i>horseman</i>: horse
        plus man, a rider, holy shit! yes, sometimes it took four hours to get
        through one episode or one youtube video until i understood every word and
        every joke, but that didn't bother me at all. i did about eight hours a
        day. i walked down the street translating every sign and every word:
        oh, a bus going past, how do you say that? i was so interested.
      </p>
      <p>
        once i wrote to a girl from my course on vk, looking for speaking practice,
        and she replied: "well, you can tell you're still learning english." and
        pointed out some mistake in my message. that same day i went outside and
        talked to a foreigner. he said, "oh, you speak so well! you've got an
        american accent." so i learned: never listen to snobs. and never judge
        anyone for their english.
      </p>
      <p>
        yes, sometimes i embarrassed myself, but mostly not. i didn't know the
        rules, of course, and <i>this stuff</i> was my most-used phrase in english.
        that's what i called any object i didn't know the word for. and why not?
        "this thing" and everyone understands you. i didn't miss a single chance:
        i wrote to people on twitter, played games online and chatted there, went
        to cafés pretending i spoke no russian or ukrainian, and if i saw a
        foreigner on the street i'd push past everyone to run over and give them
        directions. slang, different accents, all of it drove me.
      </p>
      <p>
        three months later i came to the english exam and the teacher said, "okay,
        well played, you got us in that first class. you're upper intermediate,
        here's your top mark." it was just wow. then came the plateau: i studied
        less, sank into depression, worked as an event host and in the good months
        made 15,000 roubles. then a friend showed me an opening at skyeng: 25,000
        to introduce students to the school. i simply didn't believe it. that kind
        of money, and remote? definitely scammers.
      </p>
      <p>
        "i wear crocs and my only english teacher is a homeless man on the town
        square," is what i wrote in my application. and the part about the homeless
        man is the plain truth. i called him uncle sasha. we met when i was sitting
        on a bench without a kopeck and he came over to bum money for a drink. one
        word led to another and he says, "actually, i lived in new york for ten
        years." and i'm like, "sure you did."
      </p>
      <p>
        and then, just like that, a kyiv homeless man with a divine brooklyn accent
        is talking to me about oscar wilde and toxic masculinity. i couldn't
        believe it. his story is a sad one: moved to the states, started drinking,
        got divorced, came back to kyiv and lost his flat. we talked often after
        that, and then one day i came and he wasn't there. and the next day too.
        i decided to think of it this way: he just got tired of acting, got into
        his private jet and flew to the maldives. and he remembers me there, the
        strange kid who practised english with him.
      </p>
      <p>
        skyeng hired me, strangely enough, and after that it was simple. at first
        i ran intro lessons, but within a couple of months everything changed.
        thanks to english, of course: the company came up with conversation clubs
        for students and was looking for someone to lead them. and who else should
        head a new product but the person in the habit of talking english with
        every stranger he meets? forty people applied for the role. i won it.
      </p>
      <p>
        it helped that i had learned english in such a strange way, because it
        meant i could build a product people actually needed. first i taught the
        clubs myself, then put together a team of star teachers who invented great
        topics and ran them, then i became a product manager. suddenly i had
        engineers, and i found out what kanban was, and agile, and customer
        development, and a lot of other words. the product started growing fast.
        i loved it, but within a year it was obvious that i needed to grow too.
      </p>
      <p>
        so i'm on an exercise bike giving an interview in english in my head. i do
        that sometimes when i'm working myself out. that was the moment i realised
        i want to make my dreams happen, and the simplest way to do it is to make
        them touch the company's dreams. am i doing that in the clubs? no. where
        could i?
      </p>
      <p>
        delivery always interested me: not just doing a task but delivering a
        product to people, watching it change for them. so i went to the leadership
        and said all of this. they said, "will you take on mobile marketing
        analytics?" i said, "yes, but i know nothing about it yet." great, they
        said. good luck.
      </p>
      <p>
        and there it became clear again what english is for: know it and you know
        the world. you can check out startups on y combinator, look at identity
        work on behance, learn how to build a customer journey map and what
        jobs-to-be-done means. you can learn a huge amount and grow very fast. i
        got that job precisely because i was never indifferent and never did
        anything just to do it. i want to know why. i have a lot of energy. i'm
        always interested. i never worked for money, and i have money now. enough
        to rent a house with a veranda and close tasks looking at fir trees.
      </p>
      <p>
        with a career or with english, just listen to oxxxymiron: less reflection,
        more reflexes. want to speak? come to our conversation clubs. can't talk?
        write. like games? play. all for reading? find a subreddit you care about.
        the point is that it's your choice, and it's a simple one: either you're
        afraid or you do it. and then one fine day you find you've got proficiency.
      </p>
      <p>
        the only thing that matters is seeing a direction and not doubting it. i
        know, for instance, that i'll make films, that i'll do creative work, that
        i'll certainly work at a western company, in another country. and i've
        understood that life has no destination, and if it did, we'd want to run to
        the end of it faster. but why? life isn't movement toward a goal. it's just
        a dance, and what matters in it is how you feel in this moment and how good
        it is for you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <a class="onward onward--back" href="/writing">
      <span class="arw" aria-hidden="true">↖</span><span>the other piece</span>
    </a>
  </section>
</Layout>
